# Genes as instruments: Mendelian randomization
subtitle: "How the gene you inherit at conception can act as a randomized instrument for drinking or cholesterol, and the four ways that design breaks."
tags: ["mendelian randomization","instrumental variables","alcohol","cholesterol","genetics","causal inference"]
teaches: ["methods/instrumental-variables","methods/mendelian-randomization","concepts/exclusion-restriction"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"Choosing a design, and turning an estimate into a policy","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/choosing-a-design-and-turning-an-estimate-into-a-policy.cast"}

## Wanted: a lottery for drinking
voice: female
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein et al. (Oregon Health Study Group)
      year: 2012
      finding: Oregon's 2008 lottery for a Medicaid expansion raised coverage among those drawn by about 25 percentage points.

