    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["nD","nY","a_dy","q_dy","nU","a_ud","a_uy","nZ","a_zd","sZ","sD","sY","c1","a_uz","x_uz","c2","a_zy","x_zy","c3","oregon","nG"] hidden true

    card title "Wanted: a lottery for drinking"

Does drinking raise your blood pressure, and if so, by how much?
    draw nD nY a_dy q_dy

The obvious study compares people who drink a lot with people who drink little.
    highlight nD

But heavy drinkers also smoke more and eat differently, and some people who never drink gave it up because they were already ill.
    draw nU a_ud a_uy

Each of those differences reaches blood pressure by itself, so the gap between the groups mixes drinking with everything that travels with it.
    highlight a_uy

A trial would settle it, but nobody can assign half a town to thirty years of heavy drinking.
    point at.ref q_dy gesture circle

The way out is an instrument: something that pushes drinking up or down for (@highlight a_zd@) reasons that have nothing else to do with health.
    draw nZ a_zd

Call the instrument Z, call drinking D, counted here in drinks a week, and call blood pressure Y.
    draw sZ sD sY

An instrument needs three things. First, Z must really move D: that is relevance.
    draw c1

    highlight a_zd

Second, Z must be as good as random, so that nothing in t (@highlight nU@)he grey box also sets it: that is independence.
    draw a_uz x_uz c2

Third, Z may reach blood pressure only through drinking, nev (@highlight nY@)er by a road of its own: that is the exclusion restriction.
    draw a_zy x_zy c3

These are the conditions any instrument must meet. A well-known one is Oregon's 2008 Medicaid lottery: the state dr (@highlight a_zd@)ew names at random for a limited number of places, winning moved people onto coverage, and the letter (@highlight x_zy@) itself is assumed to touch health only through the coverage it brought.
    draw oregon

    erase oregon

With all three in place, the gap in Y between the two values of Z, divided by the gap in D, is the effect of drinking for the people the instrument moved.
    flow a_zd a_dy

    quiz "A researcher proposes \"my doctor told me to cut down\" as an instrument for drinking. Which condition fails?" intro "A new case."
        right: Independence first: doctors advise cutting down because blood pressure is already high, so an arrow runs from health into Z. And the advice usually comes with salt, diet and medication, a road of its own, so exclusion fails too.
        wrong: Ask who gets that advice in the first place. Does anything in the grey box set it?
        * Relevance: the advice does not change drinking
        + Independence: advice goes to people already unwell
        * None: it is a fine instrument

So where could we find something that sets drinking by chance, long before any illness could set it?
    highlight nZ

    erase nZ

Inside each of us: a gene, dealt at conception.
    draw nG

## Nature's draw
