    card title "From gene to effect"

Turning that draw into a number takes two comparisons and one division.
    draw hd_z hd_d hd_y rule1

Take a worked example with illustrative numbers: men with no A, and men who carry it.
    draw r1 r2

The gene is our Z: zero for no A, one for carriers.
    draw z1 z2

Men with no A drink fourteen drinks a week on average; carriers drink four.
    draw d1 d2

That gap of ten drinks is the first stage: how far the gene moves drinking.
    draw rule2 gD gDl

Their systolic blood pressures average one hundred and thirty-six and one hundred and thirty-two.
    draw y1 y2

That gap of four points is the reduced form: how far the gene moves blood pressure.
    draw gY gYl

If the gene touches blood pressure only through drinking, all four points travelled through those ten fewer drinks.
    draw wald

So divide the gap in blood pressure by the gap in drinking.
    morph wald tex "\\hat\\beta = \\frac{132-136}{4-14}"

    hide wald

Four points over ten drinks: zero point four points of blood pressure for each weekly drink.
    draw wald2

Reading it as an effect needs one more condition, monotonicity: the variant must push everyone's drinking the same way, down, which a flushing gene makes plausible.
    highlight gD

Then the ratio is an average effect among the men whose drinking the gene moves, weighted by how far it moves them, and it is reliable in large samples rather than exact in small ones.
    highlight wald2 part "{0.4}"

    erase example

Real data show the same pattern. In 2008 Chen and colleagues pooled five studies and reported blood pressure by genotype, a reduced form rather than a ratio per drink.
    draw st_h1 st_h2 st_h3 st_rule s1

In men, two G copies went with systolic pressure seven (@draw e1@) and a half points higher than two A copies, with a ninety-five percent interval f (@draw ci1@)rom about five and a half to nine and a half.

The same idea then tested a belief about drinking and stroke: from 2004 the China Kado (@draw s2@)orie Biobank followed half a million adults for about ten years, and genotyped a hundred and sixty thousand of them for two alcohol variants, in ALDH2 and ADH1B.

Asked about their habits, men who drank moderately looked less likely to have a stroke than men who did not drink at all.
    draw ax1 u1 lab1 risk

The genes showed no such dip.
    draw ax2 g1 lab2

Instead, the risk of the commonest kind of stroke rose steadily:  (@draw e2@)twenty-seven percent for each extra two hundred and eighty grams a week, about four drinks a day,  (@draw ci2@)with an interval from thirteen to forty-three percent.

So the gene backed the blood pressure story, and took apart the comfortable one about a protective glass a day.
    highlight g1

## A new case: good cholesterol
voice: female
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: voight2012
      title: 'Plasma HDL cholesterol and risk of myocardial infarction: a mendelian randomisation study'
      authors: Voight et al., Lancet
      year: 2012
      doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(12)60312-2
      finding: 'Observational: OR 0.62 (95% CI 0.58-0.66) per SD higher HDL. LIPG Asn396Ser carriers (HDL +0.14 mmol/L, expected OR 0.87): OR 0.99 (0.88-1.11), 20,913 cases. 14-SNP HDL score: OR 0.93 (0.68-1.26) per SD. 13-SNP LDL score: OR 2.13 (1.69-2.69) per SD.'

