    card title "A new case: good cholesterol"

Here is a new case, from the heart: does more of the 'good' cholesterol, HDL, protect against heart attacks?
    draw axis t0_5 t1 t2 t2_5 axlab null hd_or

Each row will be an odds ratio for a heart attack: one means no effect, and left of one means protection.
    highlight null

In ordinary data, one standard deviation more HDL went with odds about forty percent lower: an odds ratio of 0.62, with an interval from 0.58 to 0.66.
    draw obs_lab obs_ci obs_pt obs_num

But people with high HDL also tend to exercise more, smoke less and weigh less, so the row could be their habits speaking.
    highlight obs_lab

In 2012 Voight and colleagues put the question to the genes, with more than twenty thousand heart attack cases.
    draw cite

    quiz "If HDL itself protects the heart, people whose genes raise their HDL should be protected too. What do you expect the genes showed?" intro "Your guess first." judge false
        right: Here is what they found, row by row.
        * Protection, like the 0.62
        * No clear effect
        * Higher risk

One variant, in a gene called LIPG, raised HDL by enough to expect odds about thirteen percent lower.
    draw lipg_lab exp_pt exp_lab

Its carriers had an odds ratio of 0.99, with an interval from 0.88 to 1.11: no sign of the protection expected.
    draw lipg_ci lipg_pt lipg_num

A score built from fourteen HDL variants agreed: 0.93 per standard deviation, with an interval from 0.68 to 1.26 that straddles one.
    draw score_lab score_ci score_pt score_num

Could the method simply be blind to cholesterol? The authors ran a check where the answer was already known.
    point at.ref "null" gesture underline

For LDL, the 'bad' cholesterol, the same approach found odds more than doubled per standard deviation, 2.13, with an interval from 1.69 to 2.69: the method sees an effect when one is there.
    draw ldl_lab ldl_ci ldl_pt ldl_num

So at least some ways of raising HDL do not protect the heart, and much of the observed forty percent was probably the company HDL keeps.
    highlight obs_pt score_pt

The score's interval is wide, from about a third lower to a quarter higher, so a modest benefit is not ruled out; what it does rule out, at this level of confidence, is the forty percent.
    highlight score_ci

One caveat: genes compare lifelong differences in HDL, which is not the same as a drug that raises it in middle age.
    highlight lipg_lab score_lab

## Four ways it fails
voice: female
level: advanced
vars:
    fs:
      value: 10
      min: 1
      max: 10
      step: 1
    est:
      expr: 0.3+1/fs
sources:
    - id: frayling2007
      title: A common variant in the FTO gene is associated with body mass index and predisposes to childhood and adult obesity
      authors: Frayling et al., Science
      year: 2007
      doi: 10.1126/science.1141634
      finding: The 16% of adults homozygous for the FTO risk allele weighed about 3 kg more and had 1.67-fold odds of obesity; the variant explains roughly 1% of BMI variance.
    - id: millwood2019
      title: 'Conventional and genetic evidence on alcohol and vascular disease aetiology: a prospective study of 500 000 men and women in China'
      authors: Millwood et al., Lancet
      year: 2019
      doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(18)31772-0
      finding: Among women, few drank; the ALDH2/ADH1B genotypes did not predict high alcohol intake and were not positively associated with blood pressure, stroke or myocardial infarction.

