    card title "Four ways it fails"

Every failure of this design is one of the three conditions breaking, and genes break them in four well-known ways.
    draw nZ nD nY nU a_zd a_dy a_ud a_uy

The first is pleiotropy: one gene doing two jobs, so a second road runs to blood pressure without passing through the glass; that is the exclusion restriction failing, now wearing a lab coat.
    draw pleio pleio_tip pleio_lab

There is a neat check: in the Chinese study few women drink, and in women the same variants did not raise blood pressure or stroke, as a second road would have.
    draw wcheck

    erase wcheck

When a second road does exist, the ratio return (@draw s_gamma s_pi parallel true@)s the true effect, beta, plus the direct effect, gamma, divided by the first stage, pi.
    draw f1

    erase f1

Back to the example: suppose the variant also lowered blood pressure by one point by its own road.
    highlight pleio

Then only three of the four points came through drink (@draw f2_truth@)ing, so the truth is 0.3 per drink, (@draw f2_bias f2_est@) yet the ratio still says 0.4; gamm (@draw signs@)a is minus one and pi minus ten, so the signs cancel.
    draw f2

Now grab the ten, the first stage, and move it down towards one, and watch the estimate run away from the true 0.3.
    explore params ["fs"] watch "Watch the first stage fall from ten drinks to one, and the estimate climb from 0.4 to 1.3."

Here it is done: at ten drinks the bias is a tenth of a point; at one drink it is a whole point, and the estimate reads 1.3, more than four times the truth.
    animate fs 1 duration 3

That is the second failure, a weak instrument, drawn as this thin arrow: it magnifies every small violation, and in finite samples it also pulls the estimate back toward the confounded comparison.
    highlight a_zd

    erase f2 f2_truth pleio pleio_tip pleio_lab s_gamma f2_bias f2_est signs

    animate fs 10 duration 1

Body weight shows the problem: pe (@draw fto@)ople with two copies of the best-known obesity variant, in a gene called FTO, weigh only about three kilograms more, and it explains a (@draw fto2@)round one percent of the differences in body mass index.

    erase fto fto2

The third failure is linkage disequilibrium: genes are inherited in blocks, so the variant you measure travels with its neighbours on the chromosome.
    draw nN ld_link ld_lab

If a neig (@draw a_ny@)hbour acts on blood pressure itself, its effect is booked as the effect of drinking.

    erase nN ld_link ld_lab a_ny

The fourth is population stratification: across a whole population, who carries a variant depends on ancestry, and ancestry also shapes diet, wealth and disease.
    draw nA a_az a_au strat_lab

Pool groups carelessly and you could discover a gene for eating with chopsticks.
    highlight a_az

Families raise the same worry inside one ancestry, and that is where economists now use these genes most.
    highlight nA

## Genes and income
voice: female
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: tyrrell2016
      title: 'Height, body mass index, and socioeconomic status: mendelian randomisation study in UK Biobank'
      authors: Tyrrell et al., BMJ
      year: 2016
      doi: 10.1136/bmj.i582
      finding: '119,669 UK Biobank participants: a genetically determined 1 SD (4.6 kg/m2) higher BMI caused a £2,940 (95% CI £1,680 to £4,200) lower annual household income in women only.'
    - id: brumpton2020
      title: Avoiding dynastic, assortative mating, and population stratification biases in Mendelian randomization through within-family analyses
      authors: Brumpton et al., Nature Communications
      year: 2020
      doi: 10.1038/s41467-020-17117-4
      finding: '61,008 siblings (HUNT, UK Biobank), replicated in 222,368 siblings (23andMe): MR effects of height and lower BMI on educational attainment were strongly attenuated within families; effects of BMI on diabetes and blood pressure were reproduced. Within-family estimates reproduced effects of BMI on diabetes and blood pressure; population MR estimates are more precise, within-sibship ones less so.'
takeaways:
    - Dealt by chance, within a family
    - Exclusion is the weak link
    - Weak instruments magnify bias

